class:: ChordStrength
summary:: Causal chord template strength
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Tonal
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/HPCP, Classes/HarmonicPeaks

description::
An original template matcher over supplied chroma, following the pitch-class/template approach surveyed by Gomez (2006), DOI 10.1287/ijoc.1040.0126. Each chord receives cosine similarity multiplied by a coverage factor: min(1, weakestChordTone/(0.05*totalChroma)). An explicit no-chord state has score threshold. Causal min-sum dynamic programming adds transitionPenalty when the state changes; optional exponential chroma smoothing follows elapsed time. The default major/minor vocabulary is unchanged; the extended vocabulary adds diminished, augmented, seventh and suspended chords.

Chroma discards octave and bass position. It cannot distinguish inversions, augmented roots separated by four semitones, or sus2/sus4 labels with identical pitch-class sets (Csus2 and Gsus4, for example). A new path breaks equal-cost ties by lower quality index, then lower root; accumulated transition costs can preserve an existing equivalent label. Missing defining tones can cause no-chord or a simpler complete template. This is a bounded template vocabulary, not a general chord transcriber. Silence immediately selects no-chord despite smoothing or transition penalty.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: chroma
A C-first Array of 12, 24, 36, or 120 nonnegative pitch-class values. Higher resolutions fold to nearest semitones where a twelve-class representation is required.

argument:: smooth
Causal exponential smoothing time in seconds, zero disables. Time follows the actual interval between trigger updates.

argument:: transitionPenalty
Nonnegative min-sum path cost for changing chord state. Higher values favour persistence; zero makes framewise choices.

argument:: threshold
Minimum similarity required to beat the no-chord state; default 0.65.

argument:: trig
A positive value processes this control block. For FFT-derived features pass chain >= 0 so each analysis frame is used once; default 1 processes each control block.

argument:: vocabulary
Literal symbol: code::\triads:: (default, major/minor/no-chord) or code::\extended:: (nine qualities/no-chord). Quality indices are 0=major, 1=minor, 2=diminished, 3=augmented, 4=dominant seventh, 5=major seventh, 6=minor seventh, 7=sus2, 8=sus4. Root is 0=C through 11=B; no-chord uses -1 for both root and quality. The vocabulary is fixed when the SynthDef is built.

returns::
Selected chord similarity, or the best frame similarity when no chord is selected. This is not probability.

examples::
ChordStrength computes the similarity score (0 to 1) between active chroma and the best chord template. When chord recognition confidence is high, harmony is unambiguously defined; during transition boundaries, chord strength dips. In this example, chord strength modulates an ambient synth pad filter, opening brightly during solid chord sustains and ducking softly during transitions. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.scope:: displays real-time chord strength.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("piano-chords.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic chords fallback
            Mix.ar(SinOsc.ar([261.63, 329.63, 392.00, 523.25] * LFNoise0.kr(0.5).range(1, 1.25))) * 0.15
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(2048), source, 0.25);
        var chroma = HPCP.kr(chain, size: 12, harmonics: 2, normalize: \max, fftSize: 2048);
        var strength = ChordStrength.kr(chroma, trig: chain >= 0, vocabulary: \extended);
        var strLag = strength.lag(0.08);
        var filtered = RLPF.ar(source, strLag.linexp(0.2, 0.85, 300, 5000), 0.3) * 0.3;
        var sound = (source * 0.2 ! 2) + (filtered ! 2);

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, strLag.clip(0, 1)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.scope("Chord Strength", ~mir2Bus, 0.0, 1.0, "sim");
});
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

